    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. Here's what I implemented for the bulk-signup warning rule.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: on `POST /api/owners`, when **more than 80** owners were already created on the new owner's business day, the response (and every later read) carries `bulkSignupWarning: true`, otherwise `false`. The existing `AcceptanceTest` creates an owner and reads it back via `GET`, asserting the field is `false` \u2014 so the flag has to be persisted on the entity, exactly like the existing `namesakeCount`.\n\nI followed the established per-owner-attribute pattern (mirroring `namesakeCount`):\n\n1. **Persistence** \u2014 added a `bulk_signup_warning BOOLEAN` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2/mysql/postgres) and a `Boolean bulkSignupWarning` field with getter/setter on the `Owner` entity.\n\n2. **API contract** \u2014 added the `bulkSignupWarning` read-only boolean property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically (no mapper change needed, same as `namesakeCount`).\n\n3. **New pipeline step** \u2014 `FlagBulkSignup` counts owners already registered on the new owner's effective business day and sets the flag when `count > 80`. Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after `countNamesakes` and before `save`, so it runs after `ResolveRegistrationDate` (business-day-adjusted date is on the entity) and before persistence (the new owner isn't self-counted).\n\n4. **Reuse instead of duplication** \u2014 the day-counting loop existed already inside `EnsureDailyLimit` (the hard \u2265100 cap). Rather than copy it, I extracted it into a small shared `DailyRegistrations.countOn(repository, businessDay)` helper and pointed both the hard limit and the new soft warning at it. `FlagBulkSignup` is a soft warning (>80) sitting below `EnsureDailyLimit`'s hard cap (\u2265100), and both count the same owners the same way.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u00b7 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
